Change files: release intent and release notes, one file per change
====================================================================

Every PR that changes a workspace crate adds a change file here. A
release (a `vX.Y.Z` tag) builds its notes from the change files ADDED
since the previous tag, read at the tagged commit, and puts them in the
GitHub Release. Nothing is deleted or committed: the files stay here as
the record. CHANGELOG.md ends at v0.10.0 and is no longer written. CI
enforces the files on every PR (`cargo rail change check` in pr.yml;
locally: `just ci-changes`). To fix a note, edit its file on main before
tagging.

Write one with cargo-rail:

    cargo rail change add <every crate you changed> --bump <none|patch|minor|major> \
      -m "Added confirmation tickets for destructive actions run by agents"

It writes .changes/<slug>.md:

    ---
    "architect-commands" = "minor"
    ---

    Added confirmation tickets for destructive actions run by agents

- The message is the release note, written for the app developer using
  architect: start with a verb (Added / Fixed / Improved / Changed /
  Removed / Deprecated / Security); name any changed type, function,
  feature flag or action id exactly.
- --bump: major -> "Breaking / Migration", minor -> "Added",
  patch -> "Fixed and improved". none = nothing a consumer would notice
  (refactor, CI, tests, docs): it satisfies the check and adds no entry.
- Breaking changes (a PR title with `!`, a removed or renamed public item):
  --bump major, and add a "Migration:" paragraph to the body (what
  changed, why, the exact steps for a consumer).
- A change to STORED DATA (a schema, a storage format, a migration that
  rewrites a vault, an index or a database): add a line to the body,

      Data-Migration: <what changes, and whether a rollback undoes it>

  at any bump level. The release's notes list it first ("Data migration")
  and its release-facts.json says `"data_migration": true`, signed with the
  release's assets: a deployer reading it (infra's release bump) never
  auto-merges that release, because rolling the binary back does not roll
  the data back. `just release-notes-test` checks the parsing.
- A revert of something not yet released: delete the original change file
  instead of adding a new one.

Versions stay semver; the highest bump since the previous tag picks the
next one (`just release-next`):

    before 1.0 (0.Y.Z)   major -> 0.(Y+1).0   minor, patch, none -> 0.Y.(Z+1)
    from 1.0  (X.Y.Z)    major -> (X+1).0.0   minor -> X.(Y+1).0   patch, none -> X.Y.(Z+1)

`just release-notes [vX.Y.Z]` prints a tag's notes, or the draft for
origin/main.

This file has no .md extension on purpose: cargo-rail parses every .md in
this directory as a change file.
